  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 329 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThursday, a weak mid-level disturbance will move across the \narea, providing increasing warm air advection (WAA), \nfrontogenesis, and differential cyclonic vorticity advection \n(DCVA) sufficient to support widespread showers and \nthunderstorms through much of the morning. Severe weather is not\nexpected with this activity given weak deep-layer shear. \nHowever, moisture across the region will remain quite \nimpressive, with recent model guidance continuing to depict \nprecipitable water (PWAT) values exceeding 1.5 inches. These \nvalues are near the 95th percentile to near the climatological \nmaximum for this time of year, highlighting just how anomalous \nthe atmospheric moisture will be. Forecast soundings also \nindicate deep moisture profiles supportive of efficient rainfall\nprocesses and locally heavy rainfall. Wherever the primary band\nof showers and thunderstorms ultimately develops, locally heavy\nrainfall will be possible. Current NBM guidance depicts the \nhighest probabilities of rainfall exceeding one-half inch across\nportions of central Nebraska. There remains considerable \nuncertainty regarding where the heaviest rainfall axis will \nultimately set up, and some adjustments to its placement are \nexpected given the forecast remains two days out. Forecast \ntrends will continue to be monitored over the next several \nforecast cycles. High temperatures Thursday will range from the \nmiddle 70s across central and north-central Nebraska to the \nlower and middle 80s across western and southwest Nebraska.\n\nFriday, another round of showers and thunderstorms will be possible \nas additional lift spreads northward into southern Nebraska, \npotentially bringing another period of precipitation to portions of \ncentral Nebraska Friday morning. Once again, moisture profiles will \nremain quite favorable for efficient rainfall processes and locally \nheavy rainfall. Confidence in the exact placement and timing of \nprecipitation remains modest at this range, and forecast trends will \ncontinue to be monitored over the next several forecast cycles. \nTemperatures will begin to moderate, with highs in the middle 80s \nacross central Nebraska and the upper 80s to lower 90s across the \nremainder of western and north-central Nebraska.\n\nSaturday and beyond, the upper-level ridge centered across the \nsouthwestern United States is expected to strengthen and expand \nnorthward across the central Plains, with forecast 500-mb heights \napproaching 597 dam by Saturday afternoon. In response, 850-mb \ntemperatures are forecast to increase into the 25 to 30\u00b0C range. \nGiven sufficient daytime mixing, these temperatures would support \nwidespread afternoon highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s across \nmuch of the forecast area. This pattern is expected to persist \nthrough the remainder of the forecast period, favoring hot and \npredominantly dry conditions as the primary storm track shifts north \nof the region.",
